AURIX TC27x blinky
==================

A minimal bare-metal image for the Infineon AURIX TC275 Lite Kit, used to
check end to end that OpenOCD can erase, program and verify the program
flash of a TC2x device, and that the programmed image really runs from a
cold boot with no debugger attached.

CPU0 alternates LED1 (P00.5) and LED2 (P00.6) at about 1 Hz. Both LEDs are
low active, confirmed against the official TC275 Lite Kit schematic and by
register read-back on real hardware.

Building
--------

Any TriCore ELF GCC works. Point CROSS_COMPILE at it:

    make CROSS_COMPILE=/path/to/bin/tricore-elf-

Flashing
--------

    openocd -s tcl -f board/infineon/kit_a1g_tc275_lite.cfg \
            -c "program tc27x-blinky.elf verify reset exit"

or simply

    make CROSS_COMPILE=... flash

After "reset exit" OpenOCD releases the target and the LEDs start
blinking. Power cycling the board keeps them blinking standalone, with
no debugger attached -- confirmed on real hardware; see "Boot Mode
Header" below for why this needs more than just the application code.

Hardware regression suite
-------------------------

../common/test-aurix-hardware.cfg is a single suite shared
across the AURIX TC2x, TC3x and TC4x families; test-aurix-hardware-tc2x.cfg
next to it sets the TC2x-specific overrides (including this image) and
sources the shared suite. See that file's header comment for exactly what
each override means and how it was derived.

Build the image first, then:

    openocd -s tcl -f board/infineon/kit_a1g_tc275_lite.cfg \
            -f ../common/test-aurix-hardware-tc2x.cfg

Boot Mode Header
-----------------

Unlike TC375/TC4D7 (which use a UCB-based boot mechanism, decoupled from
the application code region), TC2x's BootROM needs a valid Boot Mode
Header (BMHD) at 0xA0000000 to boot the flashed application standalone.
Without one, the official TC21x/TC22x/TC23x User's Manual (chapter
5.1.6.3) documents that the BootROM only takes "Internal Start" mode --
the one this image needs -- when a debugger is attached and requests
halt-after-reset; otherwise it falls back to Generic Bootstrap Loader
mode, which never runs the flashed image at all. This was confirmed the
hard way on real TC275 hardware: the exact same flashed bytes ran fine
while a debugger was attached, but the board looked "dead" the moment it
was disconnected.

gen-bmhd.py generates the 32-byte header (bmhd0.bin), and bmhd.S/tc27x.ld
place it at 0xA0000000, immediately before the application code at
0xA0000020 -- both pulled in automatically by a normal build, so this is
not something you need to do by hand. See gen-bmhd.py's header comment
for the full field-by-field derivation (citing the manual's Table 5-1)
and tc27x.ld's comment for the end-to-end explanation. Hardware-verified
three ways: byte-exact flash readback, a live register trace showing the
blink loop still executing, and a full debugger disconnect with the
board owner visually confirming LED1/LED2 blinking standalone.

Notes
-----

The image is linked at 0xa0000000 (the BMHD) with the application code
at 0xa0000020, the fixed "Internal Start" entry address for this family
(see tc27x.ld's comment and startup.S).

No watchdog-disable call is needed before the application runs: a stage 1
probe (startup-stage1.S / main-stage1.c, built with "make stage1") flashed
a free-running counter loop with no watchdog code at all and confirmed on
real hardware that it keeps incrementing indefinitely with no reset. See
startup.S's comment for the full reasoning, including why no watchdog SFR
address is guessed at here -- same finding and same approach as
aurix-blinky/tc4d7.
